            <ArticleTitle>CBP/p300 Bromodomain Mediates Amyloid Formation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>128-129</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2451-9456(17)30023-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.chembiol.2017.01.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bromodomains are protein domains that serve as &quot;readers&quot; of acetylated lysine marks and mediate DNA-templated processes. In this issue of Cell Chemical Biology, Olzscha et al. (2017) report that the CBP/p300 bromodomains mediate the formation of amyloid-like aggregates and that inhibitors specific to these bromodomains reduce the degree of protein aggregation and mitigate HDAC inhibitor-induced cytotoxicity.</AbstractText>
